          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reproduction: How Life Continues"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 209"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.087,
            0.774,
            0.124
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "species. In this chapter, we will explore how reproduction takes place in different organisms, including humans."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.135,
            0.459,
            0.157
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.1 Asexual Reproduction"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.16,
            0.774,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Asexual mode of reproduction is seen in many unicellular organisms like bacteria, amoeba, yeast and simple multicellular organisms (hydra, sponge). It is also seen in many plants. Recall the examples you studied in earlier grades."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.782,
            0.169,
            0.852,
            0.202
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4 "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 8",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Curiosity "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 13",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.23,
            0.775,
            0.385
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Many types of plants sprout new shoots and roots from their existing parts. For example, plants with fleshy underground stems, such as potato and ginger, sprout new plants without producing seeds. Money plant stem, sugarcane stem cuttings grow into a new plant, Bryophyllum (Fig. 11.1) leaves sprout tiny plantlets which eventually grow into new plants. All these are examples of vegetative propagation, which means that new plants arise from the vegetative parts, i.e., from growing parts of a plant. The key point about this type of reproduction is that it involves only one parent and hence, produces genetically identical individuals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.792,
            0.216,
            0.938,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.792,
                0.216,
                0.938,
                0.333
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_1_image_7.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.78,
                0.337,
                0.944,
                0.38
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 11.1: Bryophyllum leaf sprouts tiny plantlet"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.392,
            0.773,
            0.432
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.1.1 How is vegetative propagation in plants helpful in agriculture?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.434,
            0.775,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Asexual reproduction produces genetically identical individuals. This natural process has been adapted by scientists and horticulturists to develop several methods of vegetative propagation, such as cutting, grafting, layering and tissue culture for growing plants. These methods are widely used to efficiently propagate plants, and have significantly improved agricultural and horticultural practices. Such methods enable farmers to cultivate desirable crops on a large scale."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.562,
            0.401,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 11.1: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.583,
            0.773,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Interact",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " with gardeners working in your school garden or farmers working in a field."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.617,
            0.775,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Observe",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the techniques of cutting, grafting and layering followed by them. Discuss these techniques with them and "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "record",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " your observations in your notebook."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.674,
            0.195,
            0.692
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cutting"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.694,
            0.773,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Note the following points while observing the technique of cutting in the field."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.728,
            0.774,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Does the gardener, scientist or horticulturist cut the overgrown branches of a plant at the end of its growing season? (Different plants have different growing seasons)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.779,
            0.775,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Observe them prepare the cuttings from a plant for the purpose of growing new plants. Note the average length of the cuttings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.813,
            0.715,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) Count the number of nodes and internodes on the cuttings."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.833,
            0.693,
            0.851
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Collect the cuttings of the shoots in the morning for planting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.85,
            0.607,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Remove leaves from the lower half of each cutting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.867,
            0.773,
            0.901
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Insert the cuttings up to approximately half of their length in the soil mixed with compost at an angle of about "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "4 5   -   6 0 ^ { \\circ }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " from the soil surface (Fig. 11.2)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.9,
            0.621,
            0.919
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Water them regularly and observe the change, if any."
            }
          ]
        },
